    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">A typical claims adjuster salary at an insurance carrier lands between $50,000 and $90,000 a year, but where you fall in that range depends on three things: whether you work from a desk or in the field, which line of insurance you handle, and how far up the career ladder you have climbed.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> The average claims adjuster salary is close to the BLS median of $76,790 per year ($36.92/hour). Trainees start around $45,000 to $60,000, experienced field and casualty adjusters earn $62,000 to $90,000, and senior or large-loss adjusters commonly make $85,000 to $120,000 or more, plus benefits.</div>

<p>This guide focuses on staff claims adjusters: W-2 employees of insurers and third-party administrators (TPAs). If you are weighing 1099 work instead, see our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/independent-adjuster-salary/">independent adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="what-claims-adjusters-earn">What claims adjusters earn at a glance</h2>
<p>The BLS reported a median of $76,790 for claims adjusters, appraisers, examiners and investigators in May 2024, across roughly 365,300 jobs. That figure blends entry-level desk adjusters with senior examiners and investigators, so it sits near the middle of a wide spread.</p>
<p>Employment is projected to decline about 5% from 2024 to 2034 as carriers automate simple, low-dollar claims. Even so, retirements and turnover create thousands of openings each year, and the roles that remain are tilting toward complex work that pays better.</p>

<h2 id="desk-vs-field">Desk vs field claims adjuster salary</h2>
<p>The single biggest split inside a carrier is between inside (desk) and outside (field) adjusters.</p>
<h3>Desk adjusters</h3>
<p>Desk adjusters handle claims by phone, email, photos and video. They review estimates written by field staff or vendors, take recorded statements, set reserves and negotiate settlements. Hours are regular and the job is often hybrid or fully remote. Desk property roles typically pay $55,000 to $75,000.</p>
<h3>Field adjusters</h3>
<p>Field adjusters inspect damage in person: climbing roofs, measuring rooms, and writing estimates on site. They manage their own routes and schedules, which means long days during storm surges. Field property roles typically pay $62,000 to $90,000, and the company car, gas card, laptop and phone that usually come with the job are worth an extra $8,000 to $15,000 a year.</p>
<p>The trade-off is physical work, weather exposure and driving. Many adjusters start in the field to learn construction and estimating, then move to a desk or into management.</p>

<h2 id="salary-by-line">Claims adjuster salary by line of business</h2>
<p>Each line of insurance carries different complexity and legal exposure, and pay follows that. Typical staff ranges based on industry estimates:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th>Line of business</th><th>Typical salary range</th><th>What the work involves</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Auto / material damage</td><td>$50,000 - $72,000</td><td>Vehicle damage appraisals, total losses, rental and repair coordination</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desk property</td><td>$55,000 - $75,000</td><td>Homeowners claims handled remotely, estimate review, contents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Workers compensation</td><td>$55,000 - $82,000</td><td>Medical and wage benefits, return-to-work, state statutes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bodily injury / casualty</td><td>$60,000 - $88,000</td><td>Liability decisions, injury evaluation, attorney negotiations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Field property</td><td>$62,000 - $90,000</td><td>On-site inspections, Xactimate estimates, storm response</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large loss / complex</td><td>$85,000 - $120,000+</td><td>Commercial, high-value homes, coverage disputes, litigated files</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>For deeper line-specific numbers, see our guides to <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/workers-comp-adjuster-salary/">workers comp adjuster salary</a> and <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/property-adjuster-salary/">property adjuster salary</a>.</p>

<h2 id="texas-claims-adjuster-salary">Claims Adjuster Salary in Texas</h2>
<p>Texas is among the largest adjuster job markets in the US, and many carriers and IA firms keep large claims hubs in Dallas-Fort Worth, Houston, Austin and San Antonio. We estimate the Texas statewide staff claims adjuster median at about <strong>$76,800</strong>, in line with the national BLS median. Mid-career staff pay by metro, based on our estimates:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Austin:</strong> ~$81,400</li>
<li><strong>Dallas-Fort Worth:</strong> ~$80,600</li>
<li><strong>Houston:</strong> ~$79,900</li>
<li><strong>San Antonio:</strong> ~$74,500</li>
<li><strong>Beaumont-Port Arthur:</strong> ~$73,000</li>
<li><strong>Corpus Christi:</strong> ~$71,400</li>
<li><strong>Lubbock:</strong> ~$69,100</li>
<li><strong>El Paso:</strong> ~$67,600</li>
<li><strong>McAllen:</strong> ~$66,000</li>
</ul>
<p>Field property adjusters in Texas tend to see the most overtime. Hail, wind and tornado claims peak from March through June across DFW, Central Texas and the Panhandle, and Gulf hurricane season (June through November) floods Houston, Galveston, Corpus Christi and Beaumont desks with claims. Winter freezes can add a burst-pipe surge too, as the February 2021 storm showed. For more, see our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/insurance-adjuster-salary-texas/">insurance adjuster salary in Texas</a> guide or the <a href="/salary-by-state/#TX">Texas row on our salary by state page</a>.</p>

<h2 id="career-ladder">The claims career ladder and pay at each step</h2>
<p>Carriers use fairly standard titles, though the names vary. Here is how pay usually rises as you move up:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Claims trainee / associate ($45,000 - $60,000).</strong> A structured training program of several weeks to a few months, then a starter caseload of simple claims with low settlement authority.</li>
<li><strong>Claims adjuster / representative ($55,000 - $78,000).</strong> A full caseload and higher authority. Most people spend two to five years here.</li>
<li><strong>Senior claims adjuster ($75,000 - $100,000).</strong> Complex coverage, litigated or high-severity files, and mentoring newer adjusters.</li>
<li><strong>Large loss / complex claims specialist ($85,000 - $120,000+).</strong> The highest-value individual contributor roles, often with national travel.</li>
<li><strong>Claims supervisor ($85,000 - $115,000).</strong> Leads a team of six to twelve adjusters, approves settlements above their authority and handles escalations.</li>
<li><strong>Claims manager and above ($100,000 - $140,000+).</strong> Owns a unit or region, budgets, staffing and performance metrics. Directors and VPs earn more.</li>
</ol>
<p>Supervisor and manager figures are typical industry estimates and vary widely by carrier size and region. Some senior individual contributors out-earn first-line supervisors, so management is not the only way up.</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Professional designations such as the AIC (Associate in Claims) or CPCU are often tied to raises or required for promotion. Many carriers reimburse the cost, so ask about tuition benefits during your first year.</div>

<h2 id="benefits-value">Benefits: the part of the salary people forget</h2>
<p>A staff claims adjuster salary is only part of total compensation. Carriers and TPAs typically offer:</p>
<ul>
<li>Medical, dental and vision insurance, usually with a sizable employer contribution</li>
<li>A 401(k) with a company match, and at some carriers a pension or profit sharing</li>
<li>Paid time off and paid holidays</li>
<li>Annual merit raises and, at some carriers, a bonus tied to company and individual results</li>
<li>Tuition reimbursement for designations and licensing</li>
<li>For field staff: a company vehicle, fuel card, laptop, phone and equipment</li>
</ul>
<p>When comparing a staff offer to independent work, add these up. A $68,000 field job with a car and full benefits can be worth more than a $90,000 year of 1099 fees once you subtract self-employment tax, health insurance and travel costs.</p>
<p>Performance reviews usually focus on cycle time, file quality, customer satisfaction and accuracy. Reputable carriers do not pay adjusters for denying valid claims, and every state has unfair claim settlement practices laws that prohibit it.</p>

<h2 id="what-raises-pay">What moves a claims adjuster salary higher</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Location.</strong> Large metro areas and high-cost states pay more; check our <a href="/salary-by-state/">insurance adjuster salary by state</a> data.</li>
<li><strong>Line of business.</strong> Casualty, commercial and large loss pay more than simple auto.</li>
<li><strong>Licensing.</strong> Multi-state licenses make you useful for regional and storm work.</li>
<li><strong>Technical skills.</strong> Xactimate for property, medical terminology for injury and comp, and strong negotiation skills.</li>
<li><strong>Willingness to relocate or travel.</strong> Internal CAT teams and large-loss units pay premiums and overtime.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="sample-path">A realistic 10-year pay path</h2>
<p>Ranges are easier to picture as a timeline. Here is how one plausible career might unfold for a property adjuster who joins a regional carrier straight out of a training program:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Year 1:</strong> Desk trainee at about $52,000, handling small water and wind claims from a Dallas-area hailstorm by phone and photos.</li>
<li><strong>Years 2-3:</strong> Moves to a field territory at about $64,000 plus a company car, writing Xactimate estimates on site and working storm overtime.</li>
<li><strong>Years 4-6:</strong> Promoted to senior field adjuster around $80,000 after earning the AIC, taking larger fires and contested water losses.</li>
<li><strong>Years 7-10:</strong> Either moves to a large loss unit at $95,000 to $115,000 or into a supervisor seat at a similar figure, with a bonus.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not every carrier pays on that schedule, and raises often slow in years with heavy catastrophe losses. Changing employers is frequently the quickest way to jump a pay band, especially once you have senior-level experience and a clean file record.</p>

<h2 id="getting-started">Getting started and estimating your number</h2>
<p>Most carriers hire trainees with a bachelor's degree or relevant experience, and many will sponsor the license after hiring. Arriving already licensed makes you a stronger candidate, especially for field roles. Many candidates start with the Texas all-lines license from the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI), the most popular designated home state license nationally because of its broad reciprocity, and TDI-approved certification courses can satisfy the exam requirement. An online course from <a href="https://www.adjustingonline.com/courses/" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">AdjustingOnline</a> is one option. Use <strong>joe20</strong> for 20% off.</p>
